at machine systems, including OCR engines, LLM-based pipelines, and multimodal LLMs, remain sensitive to structural and gestalt-based perturbations. • We provide empirical evidence of a persistent gap between human visual cognition and machine interpretation across multiple AI modalities. • We establish a reproducible benchmarking protocol for evaluating robustness against AI-assisted cheating via image capture and external model querying. Key findings Limited robustness of general-purpose AI systems • OCR systems, vision-language models, and multimodal LLM pipelines all exhibit sensitivity to structurally transformed text inputs. • Downstream LLM reasoning over extracted or vision-encoded text is affected by upstream perceptual degradation. Effectiveness of perception-driven transformations • Transformations inspired by human perceptual organization (e.g., Gestalt principles) significantly reduce machine interpretability. • Human readers remain able to reconstruct semantic content through cognitive pattern completion mechanisms. Dominant mechanism of degradation • Disruption of layout consistency, character connectivity, and structural alignment is more effective than pixel-level perturbations. • Machine systems rely on brittle intermediate representations that can be selectively destabilized. Preservation of human readability • Across most transformation settings, human readability remains high while machine interpretability significantly decreases. • This indicates a separable design space between human and machine perception. Final takeaway • AI-assisted cheating in exams and programming contests increasingly relies on general-purpose AI systems, including OCR engines, LLMs, and multimodal models. • Conventional pixel-level obfuscation is insufficient against these systems. • Perception-driven transformations provide a general mechanism to reduce machine interpretability while preserving human readability. • The proposed framework highlights a broader direction 